WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.960
From improvised comedy gold&nbsp;
to genuine babbling blunders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.220
these bloopers had us sticking around — and&nbsp;
laughing hysterically — during the credits.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.200
Following his star-making debut on&nbsp;
"Saturday Night Live" in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.980
Eddie Murphy went on to have a fruitful&nbsp;
career in comedies, with notable hits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.740
like "Beverly Hills Cop" and "Trading Places."&nbsp;
In 1996, he continued that golden streak with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:27.900
"The Nutty Professor," a remake of the 1963&nbsp;
classic starring Jerry Lewis. Murphy plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.060
Sherman Klump, a morbidly obese man who, in&nbsp;
a Jekyll-and-Hyde-esque science experiment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.160
transforms himself into the overconfident Buddy&nbsp;
Love in order to win over the woman of his dreams.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.440
As he does in a number of his films, Murphy takes&nbsp;
on several roles throughout the film, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.840
other members of the Klump family. This results in&nbsp;
a couple of wild family dinner sequences that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:54.060
as funny as they are chaotic. However, the true&nbsp;
hilarity of this scene is best shown in the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.740
credits outtakes, which depict Murphy breaking&nbsp;
character as he tries to perform each part.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:04.080
Scored to the song "Macho Man" by the Village&nbsp;
People, the bloopers feature a number of fart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.135
jokes that make Eddie and the rest of the cast&nbsp;
and crew break down into fits of laughter.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:13.140
When you manage to get Jim Carrey to&nbsp;
be the face of your studio comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.820
it's usually best to just let him go wild&nbsp;
on camera. The comedian is known for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:22.680
over-the-top improvisations in films like "Dumb&nbsp;
and Dumber" and "The Truman Show," a talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:28.140
that's on full display during the end-credits&nbsp;
blooper reel of "Liar Liar." Released in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:33.060
"Liar Liar" stars Carrey as an ethically&nbsp;
challenged lawyer whose son wishes for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.040
to be forced to tell the truth for one day. It&nbsp;
provides lots of great opportunities for Carrey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.840
to shine with physical comedy, including an iconic&nbsp;
scene in which he beats himself up in a bathroom.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:46.620
The bloopers at the end of "Liar&nbsp;
Liar" reveal just how much fun it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.640
was for everyone else on set to be a&nbsp;
witness to Carrey's brilliance — his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.740
improvisations never fail to get the crew to&nbsp;
burst out into hysterical laughter. He’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.240
outstanding comedic presence on screen,&nbsp;
as well as a charismatic leading man.

00:02:00.539 --> 00:02:02.879
"No! Wait! I—(breaks down into gibberish)"

00:02:09.714 --> 00:02:13.320
Hollywood was positive about Carrey's&nbsp;
work in the film too, and he scored a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.580
Golden Globe nomination for best performance&nbsp;
by an actor in a motion picture — comedy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.260
In a true display of his range, Carrey also&nbsp;
ended up with a Globe the following year for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.040
best performance by an actor in a motion&nbsp;
picture — drama for "The Truman Show."

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.040
In 2022, Tim Allen reprised his role as&nbsp;
Scott Calvin, a family man who inherits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.120
the role of Santa Claus, in the Disney+&nbsp;
series "The Santa Clauses." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.080
the story first began in 1994 with "The&nbsp;
Santa Clause," and was then followed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:45.960
with two sequels: one in 2002 and another in&nbsp;
2006. The final installment of the trilogy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:50.340
"The Santa Clause 3: The Escape Clause,"&nbsp;
finds Scott desperate to reclaim his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.080
position as Santa after mischief caused&nbsp;
by Jack Frost causes him to lose his job.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.320
Frost is played by comedy legend Martin Short in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:01.680
the film, and during the end-credits&nbsp;
bloopers, audiences can see the magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.210
that resulted from having two comedic&nbsp;
icons on the screen at the same time.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:11.697
"Allen and Smart&nbsp;both scream in a goofy, over dramatic way,

00:03:11.697 --> 00:03:15.341
&nbsp;stop, then resume screaming. Allen laughs."

00:03:15.341 --> 00:03:19.260
Although "The Santa Clause 3" was a&nbsp;
critical failure, it performed decently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:24.240
at the box office. Regardless, it remains a&nbsp;
beloved series set around a joyous holiday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:29.459
with the end-credits bloopers showcasing&nbsp;
a similar kind of fun that was had on set.

00:03:29.738 --> 00:03:32.880
"Is my acting somehow humorous to you?"

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.280
Although many moviegoers know Zach Galifianakis&nbsp;
for his appearances in films like "The Hangover"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.360
and "Due Date," those who follow him online&nbsp;
are familiar with a different side of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.520
comedian. His comedy talk show, "Between&nbsp;
Two Ferns," debuted on Funny or Die in 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:51.600
featuring high profile guests ranging&nbsp;
from Brad Pitt to Barack Obama.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.100
When it was announced that "Between Two Ferns"&nbsp;
would make the jump to feature-length comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.480
many fans of the series were puzzled as to how&nbsp;
it would work as a longer story. Nonetheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:05.520
"Between Two Ferns: The Movie" premiered on&nbsp;
Netflix in 2019 and saw Galifianakis play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.840
fictional version of himself as he interviews&nbsp;
celebrities and tries to keep his show alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:14.700
Featuring cameos from celebrities like Brie&nbsp;
Larson, Keanu Reeves, and Benedict Cumberbatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.780
the film was subject to decent reviews&nbsp;
and compared to some of the more watchable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:23.820
"Saturday Night Live" spinoffs. Of course, one&nbsp;
of the best parts comes during the end credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.940
when we see some of the takes that&nbsp;
didn't make it into the final cut.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:31.260
Given that "Between Two Ferns" bloopers&nbsp;
are hard to come by — to the point that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.580
it's not always clear whether Galifanakis&nbsp;
is being serious or making a joke — it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.060
makes for a heartwarming ending to see&nbsp;
Galifianakis break character in between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:43.200
insults. It seems like everyone involved&nbsp;
had a blast making the movie — especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:47.580
Paul Rudd, whose improvisations turn&nbsp;
Galifianakis red-faced and hysterical.

00:04:47.815 --> 00:04:52.938
"Oh… James Franco wears corrective lenses."

00:04:55.755 --> 00:04:58.080
Will Ferrell's movies were some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.520
of the best studio comedies throughout the early&nbsp;
2000s. Hot off of "Saturday Night Live," Ferrell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.300
starred in critically-acclaimed comedies&nbsp;
like "Elf," "Zoolander," and "Anchorman:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:12.300
The Legend of Ron Burgundy." His films also often&nbsp;
contain end-credits blooper reels — like the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.220
"Anchorman" movie, which features Ferrell making&nbsp;
his castmates break during improvised scenes.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:22.080
Following the success of his earlier films,&nbsp;
Ferrell teamed up with actor John C. Reilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.700
in 2006's "Talladega Nights: The Ballad&nbsp;
of Ricky Bobby," a spiritual successor to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:31.680
"Anchorman" centered on the world of race car&nbsp;
driving. Ferrell plays the titular Ricky Bobby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.680
a NASCAR celebrity whose life is derailed after&nbsp;
he is outshone by a new driver played by Sacha&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:42.660
Baron Cohen. The rest of the supporting cast&nbsp;
includes Amy Adams, Michael Clarke Duncan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.660 --> 00:05:47.280
and Jane Lynch, but this movie's blooper reel&nbsp;
puts the spotlight mostly on Ferrell and Reilly.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:50.532
"Ninety-eight percent of us will&nbsp;
die at some point in our lives."

00:05:50.554 --> 00:05:53.314
"The darkness is creeping towards&nbsp;
you, whether you know it or not."

00:05:53.314 --> 00:05:56.640
The two comics bounce off each other&nbsp;
easily during outtakes of a dinner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:00.600
party scene and make each other break&nbsp;
during chaotic scenes. It's no wonder they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.115
reunited to make "Step Brothers" two years later.

00:06:03.637 --> 00:06:09.180
"Rush Hour" was released in 1998, sparking a&nbsp;
multi-million dollar franchise led by action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.220
star Jackie Chan and comedian Chris Tucker.&nbsp;
The two play an unlikely pair of cops tasked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:19.020
with rescuing the kidnapped daughter of a&nbsp;
Chinese politician. This film is as thrilling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.400
as it is hilarious, and audiences loved it so&nbsp;
much that they kept coming back for sequels.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:28.680
Part of what made the "Rush Hour" films such&nbsp;
amazing theater-going experiences — besides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:33.660
the appeal of the movies themselves — were&nbsp;
the end-credits blooper reels. Each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.200
three installments in the "Rush Hour"&nbsp;
franchise includes a number of outtakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:41.160
that are shown off as the credits roll.&nbsp;
They’re excellent showcases of Chan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.796
charisma on set, as he never misses an&nbsp;
opportunity to be a true entertainer.

00:06:45.796 --> 00:06:49.920
"Anything goes wrong, you bust in there&nbsp;
and act like you’re LAP— blah blah blahhh."

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.480
"He cannot speak English!"

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:57.900
Tucker is also a highlight of the "Rush Hour"&nbsp;
bloopers, even as he rarely breaks character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.800
Though Chan and Tucker may have seemed&nbsp;
like an odd pair before the release of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:05.760
"Rush Hour," these end credits prove that&nbsp;
they're actually a match made in heaven.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:12.060
A more recent film that showcases some quality&nbsp;
bloopers is the 2022 Paramount+ original film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:17.940
"Honor Society." This movie stars Angourie Rice&nbsp;
as Honor Rose, a type-A student determined to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.600
get her advisor's recommendation to Harvard&nbsp;
while competing with her classmate Michael,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.580
played by Gaten Matarazzo of "Stranger&nbsp;
Things'' fame. Her initial plan to seduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:31.380
him as a distraction from his studies goes&nbsp;
awry when the two actually fall for each other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.960
imbuing this comedy with&nbsp;
tons of heart and tension.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:38.220
However, "Honor Society" goes down a dark path&nbsp;
towards the end, as it's revealed that Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:42.540
had been manipulating Honor the entire time&nbsp;
in an effort to secure the recommendation for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:47.160
himself. Honor eventually one-ups Michael by&nbsp;
securing it instead for her friend Kennedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.300
prioritizing her friendships over both&nbsp;
romance and her own academic success.

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.560
Nevertheless, the film closes out with&nbsp;
an end-credits blooper reel that leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.240
audiences remembering the film's fun rather than&nbsp;
its darker aspects. Though it doesn't feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.160
as many outtakes or improvisational moments&nbsp;
from its cast, this kind of end-credits reel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:10.560
showcases the chemistry between the film’s actors,&nbsp;
particularly Rice and Matarazzo as its leads.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:16.500
Few end-credits blooper reels are as notable&nbsp;
as the one shown in the 1979 film "Being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:21.300
There," directed by Hal Ashby of "Harold&nbsp;
and Maude" fame. This classic film stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.200
Peter Sellers as an oblivious gardener&nbsp;
who ends up becoming a political power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.580
player after meeting a rich couple played&nbsp;
by Melvyn Douglas and Shirley Maclaine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.620
The film was one of Sellers' biggest hits and&nbsp;
garnered numerous award nominations and wins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:39.900
including a Golden Globe win for Sellers and a&nbsp;
best supporting actor Academy Award for Douglas.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.920
Though it was more uncommon at the time,&nbsp;
"Being There" ends with a blooper reel of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.640
a single scene as the credits roll.&nbsp;
We see Sellers struggle to keep a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:52.104
straight face as he dryly quotes a man who&nbsp;
threatened him at knifepoint earlier on.

00:08:52.104 --> 00:08:54.264
"What was the message, Mr. Gardener?"

00:08:55.667 --> 00:08:57.887
"Now get this honky…"

00:08:58.241 --> 00:09:06.120
Even though Sellers was seemingly a shoo-in for&nbsp;
best actor at the Oscars that year, the category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.860
would eventually be won by Dustin Hoffman for his&nbsp;
performance in "Kramer vs. Kramer." According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:14.760
Douglas' granddaughter, Sellers felt the&nbsp;
end-credits blooper cost him the award,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:18.796
revealing his brilliant performance in&nbsp;
the film as exactly that: a performance.

00:09:19.257 --> 00:09:23.700
"This is 40," written and directed by Judd&nbsp;
Apatow, also features a single scene's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:29.580
outtakes over its end credits. The 2012 comedy&nbsp;
mostly centers on Paul Rudd and Leslie Mann,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.580 --> 00:09:34.020
reprising their roles of Pete and Debbie&nbsp;
from Apatow's "Knocked Up" as they grapple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:38.220
with parenthood, relationship issues, and&nbsp;
their careers. "This is 40" also features&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:42.600
an incredibly robust supporting cast,&nbsp;
including Megan Fox, John Lithgow, Jason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.580
Segel [SEE-gul], Lena Dunham, and Chris O'Dowd,&nbsp;
but one particular star shines the brightest.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:53.280
That would be Melissa McCarthy, who in 2012 was&nbsp;
rolling on the huge success of "Bridesmaids,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.940
which was released the previous year.&nbsp;
In "This is 40," she plays Catherine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.940 --> 00:10:00.540
a fellow parent at Pete and Debbie's&nbsp;
children's school who files a complaint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.460
with the principal for their daughters' behavior.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:06.840
In the outtakes, McCarthy goes on a&nbsp;
rampaging improvisation about her violent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:11.815
urges towards the two stoic parents, which causes&nbsp;
Rudd and Mann to break character several times.

00:10:11.815 --> 00:10:18.175
"I would like to rear up and jackknife my legs and&nbsp;
kick you both in the f—ing jaw with my foot bone!"

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:23.520
It’s no wonder McCarthy became such a&nbsp;
Hollywood comedy staple in the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.840
that followed. Her appearance in "This is&nbsp;
40" was a precursor to her reign in studio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.289
comedies throughout the 2010s, including&nbsp;
"Identity Thief," "The Heat," and "Spy."

00:10:32.289 --> 00:10:36.600
"Horrible Bosses" features a powerful&nbsp;
sampling of comedic talent. The 2011&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:41.160
film centers on a group of three friends&nbsp;
— played by Jason Sudeikis, Jason Bateman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.260
and Charlie Day — whose frustrations with their&nbsp;
inept, sociopathic, and sadistic bosses result in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:50.400
them planning to murder their superiors.&nbsp;
The movie is not only hilarious, but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:54.780
surprisingly thrilling comedic romp involving&nbsp;
murder, espionage, and life-or-death stakes.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.080
Given the number of stars and laughs&nbsp;
involved, it's no wonder that this film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.920
ends with a blooper reel over the credits.&nbsp;
The hilarious chemistry between Sudeikis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.360
Bateman, and Day is on full display as they&nbsp;
make each other break down laughing. Another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.680
highlight includes Colin Farrell's hilarious&nbsp;
improvisations with a deadpan pharmacist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.080
a scene that didn't make the film's final cut.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:17.640
Viewers found the blooper reel so entertaining&nbsp;
that one was included again in the film's sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.140
"Horrible Bosses 2," which also introduced&nbsp;
the talents of Chris Pine and Christoph Waltz.

00:11:22.440 --> 00:11:27.360
Pixar earned its place in movie history with&nbsp;
a string of hits starting with "Toy Story" in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:35.040
1995. That was quickly followed up with "A Bug's&nbsp;
Life" in 1998 and "Toy Story 2" in 1999. In 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:40.140
the studio would entrust Pete Docter to direct&nbsp;
his first of many Pixar films with "Monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.140
Inc.," which takes place in a world&nbsp;
where monsters harvest children’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:47.100
screams in order to fuel their city. The&nbsp;
film thrives due to its likable characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.660
with its two leads, Sully and Mike, voiced by&nbsp;
John Goodman and Billy Crystal respectively.

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:55.620
Pixar’s animated blooper tradition began&nbsp;
with "A Bug’s Life," but "Monsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.880
Inc." was the last of their productions to be&nbsp;
released with them in the credits. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:04.409
unlike the other movies on this list,&nbsp;
Pixar outtakes aren't genuine mistakes.

00:12:04.409 --> 00:12:06.989
"Go ahead… go throw up."

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:10.566
"What, what-ah what— what did I say? What, what?"

00:12:10.566 --> 00:12:11.605
"Okay, cut!"

00:12:11.605 --> 00:12:15.960
The outtakes of "Monsters, Inc." that play through&nbsp;
the credits are fully animated and voice-acted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:19.980
including a "Toy Story" cameo and several&nbsp;
scenes from the company play "Put That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:24.180
Thing Back Where It Came From Or So Help&nbsp;
Me." This tradition, as brief as it was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:27.539
made Pixar’s characters even more&nbsp;
lovable than they already were.

